<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 1.5em;">D-MAXIMAL SETS</p>
  </header>

  <p><span data-field="page_3_paragraph_1">{{ page_3_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="definition">
    <p><strong>Definition 2.1.</strong> We call $\mathcal{A}_0 \cup \mathcal{A}_1$ a splitting of $\mathcal{A}$, and we call $\mathcal{A}_0$ and $\mathcal{A}_1$ a splitting of $\mathcal{A}$ if $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_0 \cup \mathcal{A}_1$ and $\mathcal{A}_0 \cap \mathcal{A}_1 = \emptyset$.</p>
    <p>(ii) We call $\mathcal{A}_0 \cup \mathcal{A}_1$ a Friedberg splitting of $\mathcal{A}$ if the following property holds for any $A \in \mathcal{A}$: if $W \subseteq A$ is not c.e., then neither $W \subseteq \mathcal{A}_0$ nor $W \subseteq \mathcal{A}_1$ is c.e.</p>
    <p>(iii) Given a property $\mathcal{P}$ of c.e. sets, we say that a noncomputable c.e. set $A$ is $\mathcal{P}$-free if there is no $B \subseteq A$ such that $B$ has property $\mathcal{P}$.</p>
    <p>Note that if $\mathcal{P}$ is a definable property in $\mathcal{E}$ or $\mathcal{E}^c$, then hemi-$\mathcal{P}$ is also definable there.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Theorem 2.2</strong> (Lachlan [{{ ref_2 }}]). If a set $H$ is hampsimple, then $\mathcal{L}(H)$ is a $\Sigma_2^0$ boolean algebra. Moreover, for every $\Sigma_2^0$ boolean algebra $\mathcal{B}$, there is a hampsimple set $H$ such that $\mathcal{L}(H)$ is isomorphic to $\mathcal{B}$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_3_paragraph_2">{{ page_3_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <div class="subsection-title">2.1. Working modulo $D(\mathcal{A})$.</div>
  <p><span data-field="page_3_paragraph_3">{{ page_3_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

  <div class="definition">
    <p><strong>Definition 2.3.</strong> (Hermann and Kummer [{{ ref_3 }}]). A set $A$ is $D$-hampsimple if $\mathcal{L}(A)/\mathcal{D}^*(A)$ is a boolean algebra, and $A$ is $D$-maximal if $\mathcal{L}(A)/\mathcal{D}^*(A)$ is the two element boolean algebra.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_3_paragraph_4">{{ page_3_paragraph_4 }}</span></p>

  <div class="definition">
    <p><strong>Definition 2.4.</strong> A set $A$ is $D$-maximal if for all $W$ there is a c.e. set $D$ disjoint from $A$ such that $W \subseteq A \cup D$ or $W \subseteq D$.</p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: right; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">P. CHOLAK, P. GERDES, AND K. LANGE</p>
  </header>

  <p><span data-field="page_4_paragraph_1">{{ page_4_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="subsection-title">2.2. Known examples of $D$-maximal sets.</div>
  <p><span data-field="page_4_paragraph_2">{{ page_4_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <div class="definition">
    <p><strong>Definition 2.6.</strong> (i) We say that a c.e. set $A$ is $\mathcal{R}$-separable if, for all c.e. sets $B$ disjoint from $A$, there is a computable set $C$ such that $B \subseteq C$ and $A \cap C = \emptyset$.</p>
    <p>(ii) We say that $A$ is $\mathcal{R}$-separable if, additionally, we can choose $C$ so that $C \subseteq A^c$.</p>
    <p>(iii) We say that a set $A$ is a Hermann if $A$ is both $D$-maximal and strongly $\mathcal{R}$-separable.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_4_paragraph_3">{{ page_4_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="page_4_paragraph_4">{{ page_4_paragraph_4 }}</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="page_4_paragraph_5">{{ page_4_paragraph_5 }}</span></p>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 1.5em;">D-MAXIMAL SETS</p>
  </header>

  <div class="subsection-title">3. GENERATING SETS FOR $D(\mathcal{A})$</div>
  <p><span data-field="page_5_paragraph_1">{{ page_5_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="definition">
    <p><strong>Definition 3.1.</strong> We say a (possibly finite or empty) collection of c.e. sets $\mathcal{G} = \{G_0, D_1, \dots, D_k\}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ (equivalently $\mathcal{G}$ is a generating set for $D(\mathcal{A})$) if for each $D \in D(\mathcal{A})$, there is an index $i \in \{0, \dots, k\}$ and a finite set $F \subseteq \mathcal{G}$ such that $D \in \text{gen}(F)$.</p>
    <p>We say that $\mathcal{G}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ if $\mathcal{G}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ (i.e., $\text{gen}(\mathcal{G}) = D(\mathcal{A})$).</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_5_paragraph_2">{{ page_5_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.2.</strong> (i) Generating sets always exist for $D(\mathcal{A})$. In particular, $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by the collection of all c.e. sets that are disjoint from $A$.</p>
    <p>(ii) If $\mathcal{A}$ is a $D$-maximal set, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by a finite set of c.e. sets.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.3.</strong> If a finite collection of sets $\mathcal{G}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$, then $D(\mathcal{A}) = \text{gen}(\mathcal{G})$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_5_paragraph_3">{{ page_5_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: right; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">P. CHOLAK, P. GERDES, AND K. LANGE</p>
  </header>

  <p><span data-field="page_6_paragraph_1">{{ page_6_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.4.</strong> The statement "A single set generates $D(\mathcal{A})$" is an elementary definable statement in $\mathcal{E}$ under inclusion.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_6_paragraph_2">{{ page_6_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <div class="subsection-title">3.1. Finite generating sets.</div>
  <p><span data-field="page_6_paragraph_3">{{ page_6_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.5.</strong> If $\{R_0, R_1, \dots, R_k\}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$, where $R_i$ is computable for all $i \in \{0, \dots, k\}$, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by a finite set of computable sets $\{R_0, \dots, R_k\}$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_6_paragraph_4">{{ page_6_paragraph_4 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Proof.</strong> If $\{R_0, R_1, \dots, R_k\}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$, we inductively define $\{R_0, \dots, R_k\}$. Let $R_0 = R_0$. Given the pairwise disjoint collection of computable sets $\{R_0, \dots, R_k\}$, let $R_{k+1} = \bigcup_{i=0}^k R_i$. If no such $m$ exists, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is infinite.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_6_paragraph_5">{{ page_6_paragraph_5 }}</span></p>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: right; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">P. CHOLAK, P. GERDES, AND K. LANGE</p>
  </header>

  <p><span data-field="page_7_paragraph_1">{{ page_7_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.6.</strong> If $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by an infinite collection of pairwise disjoint sets, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by an infinite collection of pairwise disjoint sets containing only computable sets, only noncomputable sets, or only computable sets.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_7_paragraph_2">{{ page_7_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Proof.</strong> Suppose that the collection $\mathcal{G} = \{D_0, D_1, \dots\}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ and that the sets are pairwise disjoint. If $\mathcal{G}$ contains infinitely many computable sets, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by a finite set of computable sets, which contradicts the assumption that $\mathcal{G}$ is infinite.</p>
    <p>If $\mathcal{G}$ contains infinitely many noncomputable sets, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by a finite set of noncomputable sets, which contradicts the assumption that $\mathcal{G}$ is infinite.</p>
    <p>If $\mathcal{G}$ contains infinitely many sets that are neither computable nor noncomputable, then $D(\mathcal{A})$ is generated by a finite set of such sets, which contradicts the assumption that $\mathcal{G}$ is infinite.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_7_paragraph_3">{{ page_7_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.7.</strong> If $\{R_0, R_1, \dots\}$ and $\{D_0, D_1, \dots\}$ are pairwise disjoint generating sets for $D(\mathcal{A})$, then there exists a finite set $F \subseteq \{R_i\}$ and a finite set $G \subseteq \{D_i\}$ such that $\text{gen}(F \cup G) = D(\mathcal{A})$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_7_paragraph_4">{{ page_7_paragraph_4 }}</span></p>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <header style="text-align: right; margin-bottom: 2em;">
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">P. CHOLAK, P. GERDES, AND K. LANGE</p>
  </header>

  <p><span data-field="page_8_paragraph_1">{{ page_8_paragraph_1 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.8.</strong> If $\{D_0, D_1, \dots\} \cup \mathcal{G}$ generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ (and $\{D_i\}$ is a collection of pairwise disjoint sets), then there exists a (pairwise disjoint) collection of sets $\{D'_0, D'_1, \dots\}$ such that $D(\mathcal{A}) = \text{gen}(\{D'_i\})$ and $\{D'_i\}$ is a subset of $\{D_i\} \cup \mathcal{G}$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_8_paragraph_2">{{ page_8_paragraph_2 }}</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="page_8_paragraph_3">{{ page_8_paragraph_3 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Lemma 3.9.</strong> Let $\{D_0, D_1, \dots\}$ be a list of noncomputable c.e. sets whose union generates $D(\mathcal{A})$. Then there is a collection of noncomputable c.e. sets $\{D'_0, D'_1, \dots\}$ such that $D(\mathcal{A}) = \text{gen}(\{D'_i\})$ and $\{D'_i\}$ is a subset of $\{D_i\}$.</p>
  </div>

  <p><span data-field="page_8_paragraph_4">{{ page_8_paragraph_4 }}</span></p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <p><strong>Proof.</strong> In a highly noneffective way, we build a list $\{D'_0, D'_1, \dots\}$ satisfying our conclusion. To ensure that this list partially generates $D(\mathcal{A})$ as described, we construct this list so that each $D'_i$ is disjoint from $A$ and every $D_i$ is contained in the union of finitely many $D'_j$'s.</p>
  </div>

</div>



<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em;">D-MAXIMAL SETS <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></div>
  <p>In Theorem <span data-field="theorem_ref_1">{{ theorem_ref_1 }}</span>, the main result of this paper, we show there is a D-maximal set of each Type.</p>
  <p>We more closely examine sets of a given Type in <span data-field="section_ref_1">{{ section_ref_1 }}</span>, but it is helpful to first observe the behavior of generating sets under splitting.</p>
  <h3 class="section-heading">3.3. Splits and generating sets for D(A).</h3>
  <p>Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_1">{{ lemma_ref_1 }}</span>. Suppose <span data-field="condition_1">{{ condition_1 }}</span> is computable, <span data-field="condition_2">{{ condition_2 }}</span> = <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>, and <span data-field="condition_3">{{ condition_3 }}</span> &lt; <span data-field="G_val">{{ G_val }}</span> (D(A)). Then <span data-field="G_prime">{{ G_prime }}</span> generates D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>).</p>
  <p>Proof. Let <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> be c.e. and disjoint from <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>. Since <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> = <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> $\cap$ <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> is c.e. and disjoint from <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>, the set <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is covered by <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> and finitely many sets in <span data-field="G_val">{{ G_val }}</span>.</p>
  <p>Corollary <span data-field="corollary_ref_1">{{ corollary_ref_1 }}</span>. A set <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is half of a trivial splitting of a simple set iff D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>) = <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> for some computable <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span>.</p>
  <p>Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_2">{{ lemma_ref_2 }}</span>. Suppose <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> = <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>. If <span data-field="G_val">{{ G_val }}</span>(A) generates D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>), then <span data-field="G_prime">{{ G_prime }}</span> generates D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>).</p>
  <p>Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_3">{{ lemma_ref_3 }}</span>. Suppose <span data-field="G_val">{{ G_val }}</span> generates D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>) and <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> = <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>. If <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> $\subseteq$ <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is a Friedberg splitting of <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>, then <span data-field="G_prime">{{ G_prime }}</span>(A) generates D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>).</p>
  <p>Proof. Let <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> be a c.e. set. If <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is not c.e., then <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is not c.e., and <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is not disjoint from <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>. So, assume that <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is a c.e. set. If <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is disjoint from <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>, then <span data-field="D_val">{{ D_val }}</span> is covered by <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> and finitely many sets in <span data-field="G_val">{{ G_val }}</span>.</p>
  <p>Corollary <span data-field="corollary_ref_2">{{ corollary_ref_2 }}</span>. If <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is half of a Friedberg splitting of a simple set, then D(<span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>) = <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> where <span data-field="R_val">{{ R_val }}</span> is not computable.</p>
  <h3 class="section-heading">3.4. Understanding the Types.</h3>
  <p>Sets of Types <span data-field="type_ref_1">{{ type_ref_1 }}</span> are particularly well understood. By Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_4">{{ lemma_ref_4 }}</span>, a set is of Type <span data-field="type_val_1">{{ type_val_1 }}</span> if and only if it is c.e. sets disjoint from <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span>. By this fact and Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_5">{{ lemma_ref_5 }}</span>, if <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is simple iff <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is Type <span data-field="type_val_2">{{ type_val_2 }}</span>. By Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_6">{{ lemma_ref_6 }}</span>, if <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is half of a Friedberg splitting of a simple set, then <span data-field="A_val">{{ A_val }}</span> is of Type <span data-field="type_val_3">{{ type_val_3 }}</span>. Moreover, sets of these Types are definable.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em;">D-MAXIMAL SETS <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></div>
  <p>Question <span data-field="question_ref_1">{{ question_ref_1 }}</span>. Is there a result similar to Lemma <span data-field="lemma_ref_7">{{ lemma_ref_7 }}</span> for the remaining Types of sets in a general setting?</p>
  <p>We finish this section with a remark on the behavior of sets of various Types under trivial or Friedberg splitting.</p>
