  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">5.2. Background on Small Major Subsets.</h3>
  <p style="text-align: justify;">
    We need some background on
    smallness and majorness for our construction. These notions will be used in <span data-field="reference_5">{{ reference_5 }}</span>
    and <span data-field="reference_6">{{ reference_6 }}</span>. One can delay reading this section until then.
  </p>
  <p style="text-align: justify;">
    Smallness and majorness were introduced by Lachlan in <span data-field="reference_7">{{ reference_7 }}</span> and further devel-
    oped in <span data-field="reference_8">{{ reference_8 }}</span>. See also <span data-field="reference_9">{{ reference_9 }}</span>, <span data-field="reference_10">{{ reference_10 }}</span>, and <span data-field="reference_11">{{ reference_11 }}</span> for more on these concepts.
  </p>
  <h4 style="margin-top: 20px;">Definition 5.1.</h4>
  <p style="text-align: justify;">
    Let $B$ be a c.e. subset of a set $A$. We say that $B$ is a small
    subset of $A$ if, for every pair of c.e. sets $X$ and $Y$, $X \cap (A - B) \subseteq Y$ implies that
    $Y \cup (A - X)$ is a c.e. set.
  </p>
  <h4 style="margin-top: 20px;">Definition 5.2.</h4>
  <p style="text-align: justify;">
    Let $C$ be a c.e. subset of a set $B$. We say that $C$ is major in
    $B$, denoted $C \le_m B$, if $B - C$ is infinite and for every c.e. set $W$, the containment
    $B \subseteq W$ implies $C \subseteq W$.
  </p>
  <p style="text-align: justify; margin-top: 20px;">
    We need the following straightforward results about small major subsets. Note
    that any c.e. subset of computable set is small in the computable set.
  </p>
